The property is **`tokenizerId`** — the assertion (at `packages/core/test/cache.test.ts`, line 220) is:

```ts
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
```

Where `bytes` is the outcome returned by submitting the assembled prompt to a second simulator built with the exact tokenizer injected:

```ts
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
}).submit(prompt);
```

So `'bytes-v0'` is the id of the injected `ExactTokenizer`, and the test verifies the simulator records that tokenizer's id on the outcome — "so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)". The same test also asserts `bytes.total` differs from the heuristic tokenizer's total while `bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` matches `heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` (the segmentation is identical; only the token accounting changes).

One precision note: this `bytes` assertion sits in the sibling test within the same `describe('Zone A — the frozen prefix (§10 rule 5, D5)')` block — the test titled "records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome…". The byte-identical/re-assemble test itself ("is byte-identical across a config-identical re-assemble and is read from cache on the second submission…", line 185) asserts prefix stability and cache-read on the second submission, but doesn't contain the `bytes` object; both tests live under the same Zone A describe.

