It builds a candidate list — the trimmed raw string, the `JSON_FENCE` fenced capture, and the substring from the first `{` to the last `}` — tries `JSON.parse` on each in order, and throws `SummaryContractError('response: no JSON object found in the model reply')` when none parse. That thrown message is the one seen in the iter7 §8 failure logs.

One precision note: the same file pattern appears twice in the codebase. `packages/core/src/summarize/contract.ts` also defines `JSON_FENCE` (line 78) followed immediately by a helper with the same job — `asJsonObject(raw)` — which is the lenient variant used by `dropTreeOwnedContents`/`parseSummaryReply` (it returns `null` on a miss instead of throwing, so the strict parser in `prompts/index.ts` owns the diagnosis). If the question refers to the function *named* for extracting a JSON object from a raw string, it is `extractJsonObject`; the twin is `asJsonObject`.

**`extractJsonObject`** — defined in `packages/core/src/prompts/index.ts`, immediately below the `JSON_FENCE` constant:

```ts
const JSON_FENCE = /```(?:json)?[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)```/;

/** Models wrap JSON in fences and prose; tolerate that, but tolerate nothing else. */
function extractJsonObject(raw: string): unknown {
  const candidates = [raw.trim()];
  const fenced = JSON_FENCE.exec(raw);
  if (fenced?.[1] !== undefined) candidates.push(fenced[1]);
  const open = raw.indexOf('{');
  const close = raw.lastIndexOf('}');
  if (open >= 0 && close > open) candidates.push(raw.slice(open, close + 1));

  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    try {
      return JSON.parse(candidate);
    } catch {
      // Next shape.
    }
  }
  throw new SummaryContractError('response: no JSON object found in the model reply');
}
```

It tries three candidate shapes — the trimmed raw string, the first fenced block captured by `JSON_FENCE`, and the substring from the first `{` to the last `}` — returning the first that parses, and throwing `SummaryContractError` ("response: no JSON object found in the model reply") if none do.

Worth noting: there is a sibling helper with the same shape in the summarize contract module — `asJsonObject(raw)` — also defined immediately after its own `JSON_FENCE` copy. The difference is that `asJsonObject` is the lenient variant used by `dropTreeOwnedContents`: on a miss it returns `null` and hands the raw reply back untouched so the strict parser (`parseSummaryResponse`, which calls `extractJsonObject`) owns the diagnosis.
